Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];
  const nouns = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    const word = words[i];
    const pos = word.match(/^(\w+)\s+(\w+)$/);

    if (pos) {
      adjectives.push(pos[1]);
      nouns.push(pos[2]);
    } else {
      nouns.push(word);
    }
  }

  return `${nouns.join(" ")} ${adjectives.join(" ")}`;
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = switchAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```

This function splits the sentence into individual words, and then filters them based on their part-of-speech. It assumes that adjectives will always come before nouns in a sentence. The function then rearranges the words to create the new sentence with the switched adjectives.

