To use the `updateAAD` method in the AEAD GCM cipher class for authentication, you need to follow these steps:

1. Import the necessary classes:

```java
import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.KeyGenerator;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;
import javax.crypto.spec.IvParameterSpec;
import javax.crypto.spec.SecretKeySpec;
import java.security.SecureRandom;
```

2. Generate a secret key and initialization vector (IV):

```java
KeyGenerator keyGenerator = KeyGenerator.getInstance("AES");
keyGenerator.init(256);
SecretKey secretKey = keyGenerator.generateKey();
SecureRandom random = new SecureRandom();
IvParameterSpec ivParameterSpec = new IvParameterSpec(random.generateBytes(12));
```

3. Create an instance of the AEAD GCM cipher class:

```java
Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("AES/GCM/NoPadding");
cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, secretKey, ivParameterSpec);
```

4. Use the `updateAAD` method to authenticate data:

```java
byte[] data = "This is the data to be authenticated".getBytes();
byte[] tag = cipher.updateAAD(data);
```

The `updateAAD` method takes the data to be authenticated as input and returns the authentication tag. You can then use this tag to verify the authenticity of the data.

5. Verify the authenticity of data using the `updateAAD` method:

```java
byte[] data = "This is the data to be authenticated".getBytes();
byte[] tag = cipher.updateAAD(data);
if (Arrays.equals(tag, cipher.updateAAD(data))) {
    System.out.println("Data is authentic.");
} else {
    System.out.println("Data is not authentic.");
}
```

The `updateAAD` method takes the same data as input and returns the same authentication tag as before. If the tags match, the data is considered authentic.

