```php
<?php

use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

class CreateUsersTable extends Migration
{
    /**
     * Run the migrations.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    public function up()
    {
        Schema::create('users', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->id();
            $table->string('name');
            $table->email('email')->unique();
            $table->password('password');
            $table->rememberToken();
            $table->timestamps();

            // Relationships
            $table->hasMany(Car::class);
            $table->hasMany(Pet::class);
            $table->hasOne(House::class);
        });
    }

    /**
     * Reverse the migrations.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    public function down()
    {
        Schema::dropIfExists('users');
    }
}
```

This migration creates a `users` table with the following columns:

- `id` (integer, primary key)
- `name` (string)
- `email` (string, unique)
- `password` (string)
- `remember_token` (string)
- `created_at` (timestamp)
- `updated_at` (timestamp)

It also defines three relationships:

- `hasMany(Car::class)`: One user can have multiple cars.
- `hasMany(Pet::class)`: One user can have multiple pets.
- `hasOne(House::class)`: One user can have only one house.

To use this migration, you can run the following command in your Laravel project:

```bash
php artisan migrate
```

This will create the `users` table and set up the relationships between users, cars, pets, and houses.

