Sure! Here's a simple Java program that calculates the total cost of your products:

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class ProductCost {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("Enter the number of headphones:");
        int numHeadphones = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.println("Enter the price of each headphone:");
        double headphonePrice = scanner.nextDouble();

        System.out.println("Enter the number of microphones:");
        int numMicrophones = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.println("Enter the price of each microphone:");
        double microphonePrice = scanner.nextDouble();

        System.out.println("Enter the number of speakers:");
        int numSpeakers = scanner.nextInt();

        System.out.println("Enter the price of each speaker:");
        double speakerPrice = scanner.nextDouble();

        double totalCost = (numHeadphones * headphonePrice) + (numMicrophones * microphonePrice) + (numSpeakers * speakerPrice);

        System.out.printf("Total cost: $%.2f%n", totalCost);
    }
}
```

To run this program, save it as `ProductCost.java` and then compile it using the following command:

```bash
javac ProductCost.java
```

Finally, run the compiled program with:

```bash
java ProductCost
```

